IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `charities-common`
IMPORT `part-1-interpretation`

§ `Charities (Jersey) Law 2014 — Part 3 — Charity Test (Articles 5-7)`

-- ===========================================================================
-- PART 3 — THE CHARITY TEST. Articles 5, 6 and 7. This is the constitutive
-- heart of the Law: everything downstream (registration under Art 11(4)(a),
-- deregistration under Art 16(1)(a), required steps notices under Art 27(1)(b),
-- appeals under Art 33(5)(a)) turns on whether an entity "meets the charity test".
--
-- Source: Charities (Jersey) Law 2014 (L.41/2014), Official Consolidated
-- Version, showing the law from 16 October 2025 to Current.
--
-- THE SHAPE OF THE TEST — three tiers, and note the two KNOCK-OUTS:
--
--   Art 5(1)  THE POSITIVE TEST — a CONJUNCTION:
--               (a) ALL of its purposes are (i) charitable OR (ii) purely
--                   ancillary or incidental to any of its charitable purposes; AND
--               (b) in giving effect to those purposes, it provides (or, for an
--                   applicant, provides or INTENDS to provide) public benefit in
--                   Jersey or elsewhere to a reasonable degree.
--
--   Art 5(2)  THE GOVERNMENT-CONTROL KNOCK-OUT — an entity that "OTHERWISE MEETS
--             the charity test, NEVERTHELESS DOES NOT meet that test, DESPITE
--             paragraph (1)", if its constitution expressly permits Ministerial /
--             States-member direction, control, or governorship.
--             This is a defeater, not a limb: it is subtracted from 5(1).
--
--   Art 5(3)  THE DISAPPLICATION — the Minister may by Order disapply 5(2) for a
--             specified entity or description of entity. A defeater on the defeater.
--
--   Art 6(5)  THE POLITICAL-PURPOSE OVERRIDE — advancing a political party or
--             promoting a candidate is "NEITHER a charitable purpose NOR a purpose
--             ancillary or incidental to a charitable purpose, IRRESPECTIVE of
--             whether it would otherwise fall within paragraph (1) or Article
--             5(1)(a)(ii)". A per-PURPOSE override that fires at BOTH gates of
--             Art 5(1)(a) — so a political purpose cannot be laundered through
--             the "ancillary" limb. Modelled exactly that way below.
--
--   Art 7(3)(b) THE IDENTIFIED-PERSONS KNOCK-OUT — the determiner "must not treat
--             one particular natural person or a group of identified natural
--             persons as being a section of the public, AND ACCORDINGLY MUST NOT
--             TREAT an entity that benefits only such a person or persons as
--             providing public benefit." The closing words make this OPERATIVE on
--             the Art 5(1)(b) question, not merely hortatory: it is lifted.
--
-- ENCODING STYLE — INERT: verbatim statute inline as bare strings (identity of
-- their context), `...` = AND-sugar, `..` = OR-sugar, layout IS the bracketing.
--
-- >>> INTERPRETATION CALL — "ALL of its purposes" (Art 5(1)(a)) is read as a
--     universal over the entity's list of purposes, and is therefore VACUOUSLY
--     TRUE for an entity with NO purposes. That is `all`'s correct semantics, but
--     as a matter of law an entity with no purposes at all cannot sensibly "give
--     effect to those purposes" and provide public benefit under 5(1)(b), so 5(1)(b)
--     does the work of excluding it. We do NOT add a non-emptiness conjunct to
--     5(1)(a), because the statute does not; we note it, and test it (see
--     `the empty shell` below).
-- ===========================================================================

§§ `Article 6(1) and 6(2) — the charitable purposes`

-- A PURPOSE is the unit Article 5(1)(a) quantifies over. Each purpose carries:
--   * the Art 6(1) heads (a)-(p) it is said to fall within — CHECKBOX flags, not
--     an enum: Art 6(1)(p) ("analogous") and Art 6(2)(f) (philosophical belief is
--     analogous to (c)) both contemplate a purpose engaging more than one head;
--   * the Art 6(2) qualifications, which GATE heads (h) and (i) and EXTEND heads
--     (d), (f), (n) and (p);
--   * its Art 5(1)(a)(ii) ancillary status; and
--   * the Art 6(5) political flag, which overrides everything.
DECLARE CharitablePurpose HAS
    description                                                     IS A STRING

    -- Article 6(1)(a) to (p)
    `(a) the prevention or relief of poverty`                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `(b) the advancement of education`                              IS A BOOLEAN
    `(c) the advancement of religion`                               IS A BOOLEAN
    `(d) the advancement of health`                                 IS A BOOLEAN
    `(e) the saving of lives`                                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `(f) the advancement of citizenship or community development`   IS A BOOLEAN
    `(g) the advancement of the arts, heritage, culture or science`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `(h) the advancement of public participation in sport`          IS A BOOLEAN
    `(i) the provision of recreational facilities, or the organisation of recreational activities, with the object of improving the conditions of life for the persons for whom the facilities or activities are primarily intended`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `(j) the advancement of human rights, conflict resolution or reconciliation`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `(k) the promotion of religious or racial harmony`              IS A BOOLEAN
    `(l) the promotion of equality and diversity`                   IS A BOOLEAN
    `(m) the advancement of environmental protection or improvement`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `(n) the relief of those in need by reason of age, ill-health, disability, financial hardship or other disadvantage`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `(o) the advancement of animal welfare`                         IS A BOOLEAN
    `(p) any other purpose that may reasonably be regarded as analogous to any of the purposes listed in sub-paragraphs (a) to (o)`  IS A BOOLEAN

    -- Article 6(2)(a) — EXTENDS head (d).
    `(2)(a) the prevention or relief of sickness, disease or human suffering`  IS A BOOLEAN
    -- Article 6(2)(b) — EXTENDS head (f).
    `(2)(b)(i) rural or urban regeneration`                         IS A BOOLEAN
    `(2)(b)(ii) the promotion of civic responsibility, volunteering, the voluntary sector or the effectiveness or efficiency of registered charities`  IS A BOOLEAN
    -- Article 6(2)(c) — GATES head (h): "'sport' means sport that involves
    -- physical skill and exertion".
    `(2)(c) the sport involves physical skill and exertion`         IS A BOOLEAN
    -- Article 6(2)(d) — GATES head (i): "(1)(i) applies ONLY in relation to
    -- recreational facilities or activities that are (i) ... or (ii) ...".
    `(2)(d)(i) the facilities or activities are primarily intended for persons who have need of them by reason of their age, ill-health, disability, financial hardship or other disadvantage`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `(2)(d)(ii) the facilities or activities are available to members of the public at large or to male or female members of the public at large`  IS A BOOLEAN
    -- Article 6(2)(e) — EXTENDS head (n).
    `(2)(e) relief given by the provision of accommodation or care`  IS A BOOLEAN
    -- Article 6(2)(f) — EXTENDS head (p) by DEEMING: "the advancement of any
    -- philosophical belief (whether or not involving belief in a god) is analogous
    -- to the purpose set out in paragraph (1)(c)".
    `(2)(f) the advancement of a philosophical belief (whether or not involving belief in a god)`  IS A BOOLEAN

    -- Article 5(1)(a)(ii)
    `purely ancillary or incidental to any of the entity's charitable purposes`     IS A BOOLEAN

    -- Article 6(5) — the political-purpose override.
    `(5) is the purpose of advancing a political party or promoting a candidate for election to any office, whether in Jersey or elsewhere`  IS A BOOLEAN

-- Article 6(2) reshapes four of the heads. We name each RESHAPED head, so that the
-- Art 6(1) disjunction below can read straight down the statute while the
-- qualifications sit where Article 6(2) puts them: adjacent, and visible.

GIVEN p IS A CharitablePurpose
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`head (d) as extended by Article 6(2)(a)` p MEANS
        "(d) the advancement of health;"
    ..  p's `(d) the advancement of health`
    ..  "(2)(a) in paragraph (1)(d), 'the advancement of health' includes the prevention or relief of sickness, disease or human suffering;"
    ..  p's `(2)(a) the prevention or relief of sickness, disease or human suffering`

GIVEN p IS A CharitablePurpose
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`head (f) as extended by Article 6(2)(b)` p MEANS
        "(f) the advancement of citizenship or community development;"
    ..  p's `(f) the advancement of citizenship or community development`
    ..  "(2)(b) paragraph (1)(f) includes - (i) rural or urban regeneration, and"
    ..  p's `(2)(b)(i) rural or urban regeneration`
    ..  "(ii) the promotion of civic responsibility, volunteering, the voluntary sector or the effectiveness or efficiency of registered charities;"
    ..  p's `(2)(b)(ii) the promotion of civic responsibility, volunteering, the voluntary sector or the effectiveness or efficiency of registered charities`

-- A GATE, not an extension: head (h) counts only for sport of the defined kind.
GIVEN p IS A CharitablePurpose
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`head (h) as gated by Article 6(2)(c)` p MEANS
        "(h) the advancement of public participation in sport;"
    ... p's `(h) the advancement of public participation in sport`
    AND "(2)(c) in paragraph (1)(h), 'sport' means sport that involves physical skill and exertion;"
    ... p's `(2)(c) the sport involves physical skill and exertion`

-- A GATE: "(2)(d) paragraph (1)(i) applies ONLY in relation to recreational
-- facilities or activities that are (i) ... or (ii) ...". So head (i) requires the
-- head AND one of the two limbs.
GIVEN p IS A CharitablePurpose
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`head (i) as gated by Article 6(2)(d)` p MEANS
        "(i) the provision of recreational facilities, or the organisation of recreational activities, with the object of improving the conditions of life for the persons for whom the facilities or activities are primarily intended;"
    ... p's `(i) the provision of recreational facilities, or the organisation of recreational activities, with the object of improving the conditions of life for the persons for whom the facilities or activities are primarily intended`
    AND     "(2)(d) paragraph (1)(i) applies only in relation to recreational facilities or activities that are -"
        ..  "(i) primarily intended for persons who have need of them by reason of their age, ill-health, disability, financial hardship or other disadvantage, or"
        ..  p's `(2)(d)(i) the facilities or activities are primarily intended for persons who have need of them by reason of their age, ill-health, disability, financial hardship or other disadvantage`
        ..  "(ii) available to members of the public at large or to male or female members of the public at large;"
        ..  p's `(2)(d)(ii) the facilities or activities are available to members of the public at large or to male or female members of the public at large`

GIVEN p IS A CharitablePurpose
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`head (n) as extended by Article 6(2)(e)` p MEANS
        "(n) the relief of those in need by reason of age, ill-health, disability, financial hardship or other disadvantage;"
    ..  p's `(n) the relief of those in need by reason of age, ill-health, disability, financial hardship or other disadvantage`
    ..  "(2)(e) paragraph (1)(n) includes relief given by the provision of accommodation or care;"
    ..  p's `(2)(e) relief given by the provision of accommodation or care`

-- Article 6(2)(f) DEEMS the advancement of a philosophical belief analogous to head
-- (c), and so within head (p). This is why an avowedly non-theistic humanist body
-- reaches the register by the SAME route as a church: through (p), by deeming.
GIVEN p IS A CharitablePurpose
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`head (p) as extended by Article 6(2)(f)` p MEANS
        "(p) any other purpose that may reasonably be regarded as analogous to any of the purposes listed in sub-paragraphs (a) to (o)."
    ..  p's `(p) any other purpose that may reasonably be regarded as analogous to any of the purposes listed in sub-paragraphs (a) to (o)`
    ..  "(2)(f) for the purposes of paragraph (1)(p), the advancement of any philosophical belief (whether or not involving belief in a god) is analogous to the purpose set out in paragraph (1)(c)."
    ..  p's `(2)(f) the advancement of a philosophical belief (whether or not involving belief in a god)`

GIVEN p IS A CharitablePurpose
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`falls within a head of Article 6(1)` p MEANS
        "For the purposes of this Law, the charitable purposes are -"
    ..  "(a) the prevention or relief of poverty;"
    ..  p's `(a) the prevention or relief of poverty`
    ..  "(b) the advancement of education;"
    ..  p's `(b) the advancement of education`
    ..  "(c) the advancement of religion;"
    ..  p's `(c) the advancement of religion`
    ..  `head (d) as extended by Article 6(2)(a)` p
    ..  "(e) the saving of lives;"
    ..  p's `(e) the saving of lives`
    ..  `head (f) as extended by Article 6(2)(b)` p
    ..  "(g) the advancement of the arts, heritage, culture or science;"
    ..  p's `(g) the advancement of the arts, heritage, culture or science`
    ..  `head (h) as gated by Article 6(2)(c)` p
    ..  `head (i) as gated by Article 6(2)(d)` p
    ..  "(j) the advancement of human rights, conflict resolution or reconciliation;"
    ..  p's `(j) the advancement of human rights, conflict resolution or reconciliation`
    ..  "(k) the promotion of religious or racial harmony;"
    ..  p's `(k) the promotion of religious or racial harmony`
    ..  "(l) the promotion of equality and diversity;"
    ..  p's `(l) the promotion of equality and diversity`
    ..  "(m) the advancement of environmental protection or improvement;"
    ..  p's `(m) the advancement of environmental protection or improvement`
    ..  `head (n) as extended by Article 6(2)(e)` p
    ..  "(o) the advancement of animal welfare;"
    ..  p's `(o) the advancement of animal welfare`
    ..  `head (p) as extended by Article 6(2)(f)` p

§§ `Article 6(5) — the political-purpose override, which fires at BOTH gates`

-- "The purpose of advancing a political party or promoting a candidate for election
-- to any office, whether in Jersey or elsewhere, is NEITHER a charitable purpose
-- NOR a purpose ancillary or incidental to a charitable purpose, IRRESPECTIVE of
-- whether it would otherwise fall within paragraph (1) or Article 5(1)(a)(ii)."
--
-- Two separate subtractions, because the statute makes two. The second is the
-- anti-laundering limb: without it, a party-political purpose could be dressed up
-- as "merely incidental" to a charitable one and slip through Art 5(1)(a)(ii).

GIVEN p IS A CharitablePurpose
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is a charitable purpose` p MEANS
        `falls within a head of Article 6(1)` p
    AND "(5) The purpose of advancing a political party or promoting a candidate for election to any office, whether in Jersey or elsewhere, is neither a charitable purpose ... irrespective of whether it would otherwise fall within paragraph (1)"
    ... NOT p's `(5) is the purpose of advancing a political party or promoting a candidate for election to any office, whether in Jersey or elsewhere`

GIVEN p IS A CharitablePurpose
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is a purpose purely ancillary or incidental to a charitable purpose` p MEANS
        "(ii) purposes that are purely ancillary or incidental to any of its charitable purposes"
    ... p's `purely ancillary or incidental to any of the entity's charitable purposes`
    AND "(5) ... nor a purpose ancillary or incidental to a charitable purpose, irrespective of whether it would otherwise fall within ... Article 5(1)(a)(ii)."
    ... NOT p's `(5) is the purpose of advancing a political party or promoting a candidate for election to any office, whether in Jersey or elsewhere`

-- Article 5(1)(a) — the disjunction that each individual purpose must satisfy.
GIVEN p IS A CharitablePurpose
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is charitable, or purely ancillary or incidental to a charitable purpose` p MEANS
        "(a) all of its purposes are -"
    ..  "(i) charitable purposes, or"
    ..  `is a charitable purpose` p
    ..  "(ii) purposes that are purely ancillary or incidental to any of its charitable purposes; and"
    ..  `is a purpose purely ancillary or incidental to a charitable purpose` p

§§ `Article 7 — public benefit`

-- The entity under test. It carries the Art 2 Entity (so the Part 1 predicates
-- apply to it), its LIST of purposes, and the Art 5(1)(b) / Art 5(2) / Art 7 facts.
DECLARE CandidateCharity HAS
    entity                                                          IS AN Entity
    purposes                                                        IS A LIST OF CharitablePurpose

    -- Article 5(1)(b)
    `in giving effect to those purposes, it provides (or, in the case of an applicant, provides or intends to provide) public benefit in Jersey or elsewhere to a reasonable degree`  IS A BOOLEAN

    -- Article 7(3)(b) — the identified-persons knock-out.
    `the entity benefits only one particular natural person or a group of identified natural persons`  IS A BOOLEAN

    -- Article 5(2) — the three limbs of the government-control knock-out. The
    -- chapeau fact ("its constitution EXPRESSLY PERMITS its activities to be
    -- directed or otherwise controlled by, or any of its governors to be -") is
    -- itself operative, and is asked separately from who the person is.
    `its constitution expressly permits its activities to be directed or otherwise controlled by, or any of its governors to be, a person of the following description, acting in that capacity`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `(2)(a) a Minister`                                             IS A BOOLEAN
    `(2)(b) a member of the States Assembly`                        IS A BOOLEAN
    `(2)(c) any equivalent of such a person in another jurisdiction`  IS A BOOLEAN

    -- Article 5(3) — the Minister's disapplication of 5(2) for this entity or
    -- description of entity.
    `an Order under Article 5(3) disapplies paragraph (2) in relation to this entity`  IS A BOOLEAN

-- Article 7(2) and 7(3)(a) direct HOW the determiner reasons; Art 7(3)(b) directs
-- WHAT the determiner may conclude. Only the latter changes the truth value, so
-- only the latter is lifted. 7(2) and 7(3)(a) are carried inert here, and are
-- ALSO expressed as duties on the determiner in the regulative section below —
-- which is where they actually bite: a determination made without regard to them
-- is a determination made in breach, and so appealable under Art 34(1) as "wrong
-- ... on ... the law".
GIVEN c IS A CandidateCharity
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Article 7(2) — the mandatory considerations (inert; see the duties below)` c MEANS
        "(2) The person determining the question must have regard to -"
    ... "(a) how any - (i) benefit gained or likely to be gained by members of the entity or any other persons (other than as members of the public), and (ii) disbenefit incurred or likely to be incurred by the public, in consequence of the entity exercising its functions, compares with the benefit gained or likely to be gained by the public in that consequence; and"
    ... "(b) if benefit is, or is likely to be, provided to a section of the public only, whether any condition on obtaining that benefit (including any charge or fee) is unduly restrictive."

GIVEN c IS A CandidateCharity
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Article 7(3)(b) — benefits only identified natural persons` c MEANS
        "(3) The person determining the question must not - ... (b) treat one particular natural person or a group of identified natural persons as being a section of the public, and accordingly must not treat an entity that benefits only such a person or persons as providing public benefit."
    ... c's `the entity benefits only one particular natural person or a group of identified natural persons`

-- Article 5(1)(b), as constrained by Article 7(3)(b).
GIVEN c IS A CandidateCharity
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`(b) — provides public benefit to a reasonable degree` c MEANS
        "(b) in giving effect to those purposes, it provides (or, in the case of an applicant, provides or intends to provide) public benefit in Jersey or elsewhere to a reasonable degree."
    ... c's `in giving effect to those purposes, it provides (or, in the case of an applicant, provides or intends to provide) public benefit in Jersey or elsewhere to a reasonable degree`
    AND "(7(3)(b)) ... and accordingly must not treat an entity that benefits only such a person or persons as providing public benefit."
    ... NOT `Article 7(3)(b) — benefits only identified natural persons` c

§§ `Article 5(1) — the positive test`

GIVEN c IS A CandidateCharity
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`(a) — all of its purposes are charitable or purely ancillary` c MEANS
        "(a) all of its purposes are - (i) charitable purposes, or (ii) purposes that are purely ancillary or incidental to any of its charitable purposes; and"
    ... all `is charitable, or purely ancillary or incidental to a charitable purpose` (c's purposes)

GIVEN c IS A CandidateCharity
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Article 5(1) — the positive test` c MEANS
        "(1) An entity meets the charity test if -"
    ... `(a) — all of its purposes are charitable or purely ancillary` c
    AND `(b) — provides public benefit to a reasonable degree` c

§§ `Article 5(2) and 5(3) — the government-control knock-out, and its disapplication`

GIVEN c IS A CandidateCharity
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Article 5(2) — constitution expressly permits government direction or governorship` c MEANS
        "(2) An entity that otherwise meets the charity test, nevertheless does not meet that test, despite paragraph (1), if its constitution expressly permits its activities to be directed or otherwise controlled by, or any of its governors to be -"
    ... c's `its constitution expressly permits its activities to be directed or otherwise controlled by, or any of its governors to be, a person of the following description, acting in that capacity`
    AND     "(a) a Minister;"
        ..  c's `(2)(a) a Minister`
        ..  "(b) a member of the States Assembly; or"
        ..  c's `(2)(b) a member of the States Assembly`
        ..  "(c) any equivalent of such a person in another jurisdiction,"
        ..  c's `(2)(c) any equivalent of such a person in another jurisdiction`
    AND "acting in that capacity."

-- Article 5(3) is a defeater on the defeater: "The Minister may by Order disapply
-- paragraph (2) in relation to any entity or description of entity specified in
-- the Order." So the knock-out BITES only where no such Order applies.
GIVEN c IS A CandidateCharity
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the Article 5(2) knock-out bites` c MEANS
        `Article 5(2) — constitution expressly permits government direction or governorship` c
    AND "(3) The Minister may by Order disapply paragraph (2) in relation to any entity or description of entity specified in the Order."
    ... NOT c's `an Order under Article 5(3) disapplies paragraph (2) in relation to this entity`

§§ `Article 5 — MEETS THE CHARITY TEST`

-- The whole of Part 3 in four lines: the positive test, minus the knock-out.
GIVEN c IS A CandidateCharity
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`meets the charity test` c MEANS
        `Article 5(1) — the positive test` c
    AND NOT `the Article 5(2) knock-out bites` c

§§ `Articles 5(4)-(8) and 7 — the Commissioner's guidance duties (the regulative layer)`

-- Part 3 is not only constitutive. It imposes a chain of DUTIES about the guidance
-- that governs the test, and about how the test is to be determined. These are
-- genuine obligations with genuine addressees, so they are modelled in the
-- regulative layer rather than flattened into booleans.
--
-- Deadlines: the statute says "within a reasonable time" (5(7)) and "as soon as
-- practicable" (5(8)) — it does NOT give a number. We use WITHIN 90 and WITHIN 30
-- as REVIEWABLE PLACEHOLDERS for those open-textured standards, and flag them here
-- rather than burying the choice. A determination of what is "reasonable" or
-- "practicable" is a question of fact for the tribunal or court; the model simply
-- has to pick something to make the trace executable.

-- Article 5(4) — the duty to publish and maintain guidance on the charity test.
-- Article 7(4) — that guidance "must in particular give guidance on the
-- determination of the question of whether an entity provides or intends to
-- provide public benefit".
`Article 5(4) and 7(4) — the Commissioner's guidance duty` MEANS
    PARTY Commissioner MUST `publish and maintain guidance on the charity test` WITHIN 90

-- Article 5(6) to (8) — the consultation and laying chain. Each step is the
-- consequence of the last: consult, then publish a report, then provide both to
-- the Minister, who must then lay them before the States.
`Articles 5(6) to (8) — the consultation and laying chain` MEANS
    PARTY Commissioner MUST `consult before issuing or amending guidance` WITHIN 90
    HENCE PARTY Commissioner MUST `publish a report on the consultation` WITHIN 30
          HENCE PARTY Commissioner MUST `provide the guidance and report to the Minister` WITHIN 30
                HENCE PARTY Minister MUST `lay the guidance and report before the States` WITHIN 30

-- Article 7(2) — the two mandatory considerations, as duties on the determiner.
-- Modelled on the Commissioner (Art 7(1) applies them equally to the tribunal and
-- the court).
`Article 7(2) — the determiner's duty to have regard` MEANS
    PARTY Commissioner MUST `have regard to the comparison of benefit and disbenefit` WITHIN 30
    RAND
    PARTY Commissioner MUST `have regard to whether any condition on obtaining the benefit is unduly restrictive` WITHIN 30

-- Article 7(3)(a) — a PROHIBITION on the determiner's reasoning, not on the world:
-- "must not presume any particular charitable purpose to be for the public benefit".
-- SHANT is exactly right: performing the act is the breach.
`Article 7(3)(a) — no presumption of public benefit` MEANS
    PARTY Commissioner SHANT `presume a particular charitable purpose to be for the public benefit` WITHIN 30

§ `Tests — Part 3`

§§ `Purposes`

`the advancement of education` MEANS CharitablePurpose WITH
    description                                                     IS "running a school"
    `(a) the prevention or relief of poverty`                       IS FALSE
    `(b) the advancement of education`                              IS TRUE
    `(c) the advancement of religion`                               IS FALSE
    `(d) the advancement of health`                                 IS FALSE
    `(e) the saving of lives`                                       IS FALSE
    `(f) the advancement of citizenship or community development`   IS FALSE
    `(g) the advancement of the arts, heritage, culture or science`  IS FALSE
    `(h) the advancement of public participation in sport`          IS FALSE
    `(i) the provision of recreational facilities, or the organisation of recreational activities, with the object of improving the conditions of life for the persons for whom the facilities or activities are primarily intended`  IS FALSE
    `(j) the advancement of human rights, conflict resolution or reconciliation`  IS FALSE
    `(k) the promotion of religious or racial harmony`              IS FALSE
    `(l) the promotion of equality and diversity`                   IS FALSE
    `(m) the advancement of environmental protection or improvement`  IS FALSE
    `(n) the relief of those in need by reason of age, ill-health, disability, financial hardship or other disadvantage`  IS FALSE
    `(o) the advancement of animal welfare`                         IS FALSE
    `(p) any other purpose that may reasonably be regarded as analogous to any of the purposes listed in sub-paragraphs (a) to (o)`  IS FALSE
    `(2)(a) the prevention or relief of sickness, disease or human suffering`  IS FALSE
    `(2)(b)(i) rural or urban regeneration`                         IS FALSE
    `(2)(b)(ii) the promotion of civic responsibility, volunteering, the voluntary sector or the effectiveness or efficiency of registered charities`  IS FALSE
    `(2)(c) the sport involves physical skill and exertion`         IS FALSE
    `(2)(d)(i) the facilities or activities are primarily intended for persons who have need of them by reason of their age, ill-health, disability, financial hardship or other disadvantage`  IS FALSE
    `(2)(d)(ii) the facilities or activities are available to members of the public at large or to male or female members of the public at large`  IS FALSE
    `(2)(e) relief given by the provision of accommodation or care`  IS FALSE
    `(2)(f) the advancement of a philosophical belief (whether or not involving belief in a god)`  IS FALSE
    `purely ancillary or incidental to any of the entity's charitable purposes`     IS FALSE
    `(5) is the purpose of advancing a political party or promoting a candidate for election to any office, whether in Jersey or elsewhere`  IS FALSE

#ASSERT `is a charitable purpose` `the advancement of education`

-- Article 6(2)(c) GATE — chess club: "the advancement of public participation in
-- sport", but chess involves no physical skill and exertion. Head (h) is closed.
`chess, as a sport` MEANS CharitablePurpose WITH
    description                                                     IS "public participation in competitive chess"
    `(a) the prevention or relief of poverty`                       IS FALSE
    `(b) the advancement of education`                              IS FALSE
    `(c) the advancement of religion`                               IS FALSE
    `(d) the advancement of health`                                 IS FALSE
